  "notes": "RTU ONLY as shipped: the stock LuxPower/EG4 WiFi dongle (TCP port 8000) uses a proprietary framing wrapper around Modbus, reverse-engineered by lux-mon's PROTOCOL.md — it is NOT plain Modbus/TCP and this firmware's InverterMap::Reader (ModbusTcpClient) cannot talk to it directly (same class of problem Deye's dongle needed a dedicated SolarmanV5Client for). Use this map only over direct RS485 (USB adapter or a genuine third-party Modbus/TCP<->RTU gateway) — if you have one, change transport.type to \"tcp\" and set the gateway's IP/port before uploading. LuxPower and EG4 (and other rebrands) share one register layout — confirmed by ant0nkr/luxpower-ha-integration, which cites LuxPower's own protocol PDF (06-01-01-PTC-Luxpower MODBUS Protocol_2024.04.26.pdf) and the EG4-18KPV PDF; SNA (off-grid) vs LXP (grid-tied) is a firmware/model distinction, not a different address map, so one file covers both. No native signed grid_power/battery_power register exists in this protocol (only unsigned split pairs) — same computed-field limitation already documented for the Growatt GEN3/4 and SMA-native maps in this repo; ships as grid_export_power/grid_import_power and battery_charge_power/battery_discharge_power, TELEMETRY ONLY until InverterMapReader gains an A-minus-B computed field. battery_soc (input register 5) is deliberately OMITTED: the register packs SOC in the low byte and SOH in the high byte of one uint16 (confirmed independently by celsworth/lxp-bridge's Rust decoder and a DIY Solar Forum capture: soc = reg5 & 0xFF, soh = reg5 >> 8) — this schema has no bitmask/sub-byte extraction feature, and reading it as a plain scaled uint16 would report a wildly wrong percentage whenever SOH is populated. Do not invent a scale to work around it; wait for a firmware bitmask feature. Holding register 66 (AC_CHARGE_POWER_CMD) and any 'charge_first_soc_limit'-style field are also omitted for the same class of reason — hoang-rio/lux-web-viewer documents them holding their value in the high byte of the word, decoded via >>8/<<8 by client code, which this schema cannot express either. 32-bit energy counters are low-word-then-high-word at address N/N+1 (byte_order: big_endian_word_swap), the same convention already used by this project's Deye maps. Sources: celsworth/lxp-bridge doc/inputs.md@19854ee88718dd3444d18fa94e36ace1fba177b0 and doc/LXP_REGISTERS.txt@8df104aa35608065e0d683332e1e151a950a52b8 (field semantics, holding-register names, widely deployed reference implementation) cross-checked against jmewing/lux-mon docs/reference/luxpower-ha-register-map.md@007421765b6260d2bcfc08c4da6af8e381ed8443 (numeric addresses, consolidated from ant0nkr/luxpower-ha-integration v1.0.0, itself citing the official LuxPower protocol PDF) and docs/reference/eg4-bridge/doc/eg4_registers.json@5701e003ffa6071ab25cf62aaac3cadba1171e50 (independent numeric cross-check, same addresses). Three independent projects agree on this register sequence.",
